  "finding": "Both runtimes retain the useful four-by-four feature-weighted start at zero/one-second delay, and skip it at two/three-second delay. The latter fits use four-by-three cuts and visibly lose boundary puzzle tabs. The native failure has the same skipped-init signature; hardware contention is not established by this experiment.",
  "visualReview": {
    "inspected": true,
    "image": {
      "path": "tmp/heart-learning/jigsaw-scheduling-v1/comparison.png",
      "sha256": "368db959bc338771c079765060af195e1f175530651228b028ad0dfe2f7ddb04",
      "bytes": 128335
    },
    "notes": "All eight renderings were inspected side by side. Four-by-four fits retain sharper tabs; four-by-three fits flatten several upper/lower boundary features. Faster tracing is insufficient to remove the layout-selection discontinuity, and error does not improve uniformly across injected delays."
  },
  "nextWork": "Bound and measure feature-layout initialization itself so a modest setup delay need not skip the useful proposal entirely. Preserve original-mask, geometry and paper acceptance; evaluate on the full corpus before integration.",
